(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-dermatology-medications-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology Medications Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which set gives the MAJOR indicators of atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Nikolsky sign, mucosal erosion and a preceding fever","Comedones, papulopustular lesions, and nodules","Pruritus, typical distribution, chronicity, family history","Raised immunoglobulin E, positive allergy skin tests, and dry patchy scales"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Raised immunoglobulin E, positive skin tests, dry patchy scales and infection susceptibility are the minor ones. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why is topical acne therapy applied to the whole area rather than to spots?","choices":["Spot application increases the risk of systemic absorption","The vehicle is only stable when spread thinly over a wide area","Individual lesions are already resolving by the time they are visible","Topical therapy only works where it is applied"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that is why the whole affected area is treated rather than visible lesions alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How are acne lesions divided?","choices":["Noninflammatory comedones; inflammatory papules and nodules","Noninflammatory as papules; inflammatory as comedones","Superficial as pustular; deep as comedonal","Acute as nodules and pustules; chronic as comedones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That split is what decides whether topical or systemic therapy is chosen. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the topical azole antifungals?","choices":["They inhibit ergosterol production directly","They inhibit uptake of molecular precursors at the fungal cell wall","They bind fungal transfer RNA to stop protein synthesis","They inhibit fungal cytochrome P450, preventing cell wall formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the named agents are clotrimazole, ketoconazole, miconazole and sertaconazole. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which patient should not be given a topical retinoid?","choices":["A patient taking an oral contraceptive","A patient over sixty years of age","A patient who is pregnant","A patient with a history of eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Retinoids are avoided during pregnancy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the depot effect, and what does it allow?","choices":["Repeated applications saturate hair follicles and shut down absorption","Skin acts as a reservoir, allowing once daily dosing","Stored in fat and released on exercise","The vehicle evaporates and leaves crystals that dissolve slowly in sweat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The reservoir is what lets a short acting drug be given less often than its half-life suggests. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why is a sedating antihistamine advised for a child with atopic dermatitis?","choices":["To reverse the skin barrier dysfunction","To treat the secondary skin infections","To lower serum immunoglobulin E","To reduce night-time itching"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The sedating property is being used for the itch that disrupts sleep. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which agents are the gold standard for atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Sedating antihistamines","Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators","Oral corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with the agent chosen by severity and by site of disease. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is mupirocin used for beyond ordinary skin infection?","choices":["Eliminating nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Eliminating vulvovaginal candidiasis in women","Treating recurrent orolabial herpes simplex","Treating onychomycosis of the toenails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its coverage is most gram positive aerobes, especially meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic is preferred for acne, and what is happening to the alternative?","choices":["Mupirocin preferred; bacitracin losing efficacy","Neomycin is preferred; polymyxin B is losing efficacy","Clindamycin preferred; erythromycin losing efficacy","Erythromycin is preferred; clindamycin is losing efficacy to resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and both are noted to lack systemic side effects. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Occlusion increases efficacy. What is flagged alongside it?","choices":["It reliably shortens the required duration of therapy","It prevents the vehicle from exerting any therapeutic effect of its own","It makes regional variation in penetration irrelevant","It raises the question of increased toxicity as well"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. pairs the efficacy gain with a query about toxicity rather than presenting it as free benefit. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Pustular inflammation on the trunk weeks after starting a steroid. What happens when it stops?","choices":["No change, because the eruption is independent of the drug","An initial worsening, because inflammation increases","Immediate and complete resolution of the eruption","Conversion of the pustules into open comedones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and warning the patient of this in advance is what stops them abandoning the plan. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which systemic effects do topical corticosteroids cause?","choices":["Raised serum lipids and brittle nails","Photosensitivity, sunburn, desquamation and stinging","Adrenal suppression, hyperglycaemia, glaucoma, cataracts","Hepatotoxicity, nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That a topical agent can do all of this is the point of the potency and duration warnings. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which organism proliferates in the anaerobic conditions of pooled sebum?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Malassezia furfur","Streptococcus pyogenes","Propionibacterium acnes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pooled sebum creates anaerobic conditions in which this organism proliferates. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which benzoyl peroxide effect must be raised before starting it?","choices":["It stains the skin orange for the duration of therapy","It can bleach hair, clothing and bedding","It causes a permanent blue-grey discolouration of the skin","It commonly causes systemic hypersensitivity reactions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the practical warning that prevents a patient ruining clothes and abandoning the drug. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How often is imiquimod applied?","choices":["Two to five times per week","Twice daily without interruption","Once monthly","Only at night, because it is photolabile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is a weekly schedule rather than a daily one. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which two groups must not receive tetracyclines?","choices":["Children under eight years and pregnant women","Adolescents and the elderly","Patients with eczema and patients with psoriasis","Patients taking oral contraceptives and patients on spironolactone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the two. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which pigmentary adverse effect does azelaic acid cause?","choices":["Hypopigmentation","Hyperpigmentation of sun-exposed areas","Slate-grey discolouration of the nail beds","Yellow staining of the sclera"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is listed with mild skin irritation and dryness. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which antiandrogen is used for acne?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Cyproterone","Spironolactone","Finasteride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and a gel formulation is available alongside oral use. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What happens to benzoyl peroxide after it penetrates the stratum corneum?","choices":["It remains unchanged in the skin and works purely by oxidising sebum within the follicle","It is converted to benzoic acid, which is active against Propionibacterium acnes","It is converted to salicylic acid, which is keratinolytic","It is converted to retinoic acid, which corrects keratinization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It crosses unchanged and is converted once through. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A patient is starting a topical azole. How long is the course?","choices":["Treatment must continue for at least six months","Treatment is generally prolonged, on the order of two to three weeks","A single application is usually curative","Treatment continues until the rash disappears, then stops immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and stopping early is how a treated infection returns. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which is a LOCAL adverse effect of topical steroids, not a systemic one?","choices":["Adrenal suppression","Cataracts","Growth retardation in children","Skin atrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Local effects are skin atrophy, acne, rosacea and allergic dermatitis related to the vehicle. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A lesion is oozing, vesicular and crusting. Which vehicle suits it?","choices":["The middle of the range, regardless of the lesion","Vehicle choice is not linked to lesion morphology","The drying end of the range","The least drying end of the range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oozing, vesiculation and crusting are matched to drying vehicles; scaling, lichenification and xerosis to the least drying. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Most topical acne agents irritate. What does that cause, and how should you start?","choices":["It confirms the drug is working, so maintain the starting strength","Switch straight to systemic therapy","It is prevented entirely by using an alcoholic solution","Patients stop; start low and increase gradually"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticipating the irritation is what keeps a patient on therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why is tretinoin applied at night?","choices":["It is photolabile","Sebum production peaks overnight","It stains clothing if applied during the day","It must be applied at the same time as benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Being degraded by light is the reason for nightly application, distinct from the photosensitivity it also causes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the anti-inflammatory action of tetracyclines in acne?","choices":["They stimulate macrophages to release tumour necrosis factor alpha","They chelate calcium ions, which prevents neutrophil and monocyte chemotaxis","They stabilise mast cell membranes and block histamine release","They inhibit the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is an anti-inflammatory action separate from killing the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which agent is class 1, superpotent?","choices":["Triamcinolone acetonide cream","Clobetasol propionate","Hydrocortisone","Desonide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, listed in class 1 in cream, ointment, lotion, spray, shampoo and foam forms. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are topical acyclovir and penciclovir used for, and what are they?","choices":["Recurrent orolabial herpes simplex infection; they are synthetic guanine analogues","External and perianal warts; they are immunomodulators","Vulvovaginal candidiasis; they are azoles","Actinic keratoses and basal cell carcinoma; retinoids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, active against herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which gap in coverage does tolnaftate have?","choices":["It has no activity below the stratum corneum","It has no activity on the scalp","It has no Candida activity","It has no dermatophyte activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and nystatin is the agent pairs with candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does imiquimod do?","choices":["Drives interferon alpha and tumour necrosis factor release","It inhibits T cell, mast cell and keratinocyte activation","It is converted to benzoic acid after crossing the stratum corneum","Corrects keratinization, cuts bacteria and inflammation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is an immunomodulator rather than a directly cytotoxic agent. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"}]};
